      "text": "\"That's not me,\" she said, finally, after a silence long enough that Asha almost filled it herself. \"That's Meera. My sister. My twin, if you want the ugly word for it, though we stopped using it years ago.\""
    },
    {
      "id": "ex4",
      "text": "\"She was in the city that week. Nobody knew, because nobody was supposed to know -- we haven't spoken in eight years, and she doesn't exactly announce herself. She borrowed my jacket off the back of a chair at the union hall because it was raining and hers was soaked through, and then that whole business with the evidence bag happened, and everyone assumed the woman in my jacket diving off my usual spot was me, because who else would it be?\""
    },
    {
      "id": "ex5",
      "text": "\"I was twenty feet up the wall the entire time, Asha. I couldn't even watch. I have never been able to make myself go near open water since the Rani, and Meera is the only person alive who can dive like that without thinking twice, because she was the one who pulled three people out of the wreck with her own hands the night it happened, and I was the one who couldn't move.\""
    },
    {
      "id": "ex6",
      "text": "Asha sat back, recalibrating six weeks of assumptions in real time."
    },
    {
      "id": "ex7",
      "text": "\"Why didn't you say something sooner?\""
    },
    {
      "id": "ex8",
      "text": "\"Because the moment I say Meera was in the city,\" Tara said, \"everyone starts asking why, and I am not ready for anyone to ask why yet.\""
    }
  ]
}

